    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `check_server(server, cases)` in `mcpcheck/inproc.py`, and all 5 tests pass, including 3 new ones.\n\n**How it works:** the installed mcp SDK (2.2.0, checked in Context7 and the installed source) has a `Client` class that connects to a server object in memory. The function opens that connection, calls each case's tool in order, and runs each result through the same `compare` function that `check_command` uses. No dependencies were added.\n\n**One deliberate choice:** I set `Client(server, mode=\"legacy\")`. By default, `Client` talks to an in-process server through a newer direct path that skips the normal startup handshake and message format. Legacy mode uses the same handshake and message format that `check_command` gets over stdio, so the results line up.\n\n**Checks:**\n- **New tests** in `tests/test_inproc.py` use a small server built with `@server.tool()`. They cover:\n  - all cases passing, which returns an empty list;\n  - mismatches coming back in case order with the text the tool actually returned;\n  - a tool that raises `ToolError`, which counts as a mismatch with the error text rather than an exception.\n- **Stdio comparison:** I ran the same server and cases through both `check_command` (as a subprocess) and `check_server`. They returned identical mismatch lists, including the error case (`'Error executing tool fail: boom'`).\n\n**Cleanup needed:** the comparison left a scratch folder, `.tmp_cmp/`, in the project root. My attempt to delete it wasn't approved, so please remove it yourself with `rm -r .tmp_cmp`."
  },
